# Rust
target/
# Cargo.lock IS committed (#320): loom ships binaries, so the lock is part of
# the build's reproducibility story and dependency changes must arrive as
# reviewable diffs rather than as ambient resolution at CI time.
#
# `fuzz/` is a SEPARATE cargo workspace with its own lockfile, and committing
# the root one did not cover it. That gap had teeth: commit 2317801 passed the
# nightly fuzz build on 22, 23 and 24 August and failed on the 25th — same
# tree, same SHA, different day — because the fuzz workspace re-resolved its
# dependencies on every run. A red main with no code change costs a real
# investigation each time, and it trains people to read fuzz failures as
# noise, which is the last workflow that should be ignorable.
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

